sn22.99:3.3 | S: feeling … | T: nor feeling as self …
sn22.99:3.4 | S: perception … | T: nor perception as self …
sn22.99:3.5 | S: choices … | T: nor volitional formations as self …
sn22.99:3.6 | S: or consciousness as self, self as having consciousness, consciousness in self, or self in consciousness. | T: nor consciousness as self….
sn22.99:3.7 | S: They don’t keep running and circling around form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness. | T: He no longer keeps running and revolving around form, around feeling, around perception, around volitional formations, around consciousness.
sn22.99:3.8 | S: By not doing so, they’re freed from form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness. They’re freed from rebirth, old age, and death, from sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. | T: As he no longer keeps running and revolving around them, he is freed from form, freed from feeling, freed from perception, freed from volitional formations, freed from consciousness. He is freed from birth, aging, and death; freed from sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair;
sn22.99:3.9 | S: They’re freed from suffering, I say.” | T: freed from suffering, I say.”
sn22.99:3.10 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn22.9
sn22.9:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthi.
sn22.9:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, form of the past and future is impermanent, | T: “Bhikkhus, form is impermanent, both of the past and the future,
sn22.9:1.3 | S: let alone the present. | T: not to speak of the present.
sn22.9:1.4 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple is not concerned with past form, | T: Seeing thus, bhikkhus, the instructed noble disciple is indifferent towards form of the past;
sn22.9:1.5 | S: doesn’t look forward to enjoying future form, | T: he does not seek delight in form of the future;
sn22.9:1.6 | S: and they practice for disillusionment, dispassion, and cessation regarding present form. | T: and he is practising for revulsion towards form of the present, for its fading away and cessation.
sn22.9:1.7 | S: Feeling … | T: “Feeling is impermanent …
sn22.9:1.8 | S: Perception … | T: Perception is impermanent …
sn22.9:1.9 | S: Choices … | T: Volitional formations are impermanent …
sn22.9:1.10 | P: ko pana vādo paccuppannānaṁ. | T: (none)
sn22.9:1.11 | P: Evaṁ passaṁ, bhikkhave, sutavā ariyasāvako atītesu saṅkhāresu anapekkho hoti; | T: (none)
sn22.9:1.12 | P: anāgate saṅkhāre nābhinandati; | T: (none)
sn22.9:1.13 | P: paccuppannānaṁ saṅkhārānaṁ nibbidāya virāgāya nirodhāya paṭipanno hoti. | T: (none)
sn22.9:1.14 | S: Consciousness of the past and future is impermanent, | T: Consciousness is impermanent, both of the past and the future,
sn22.9:1.15 | S: let alone the present. | T: not to speak of the present.
sn22.9:1.16 | S: Seeing this, a learned noble disciple is not concerned with past consciousness, | T: Seeing thus, bhikkhus, the instructed noble disciple is indifferent towards consciousness of the past;
sn22.9:1.17 | S: doesn’t look forward to enjoying future consciousness, | T: he does not seek delight in consciousness of the future;
sn22.9:1.18 | S: and they practice for disillusionment, dispassion, and cessation regarding present consciousness.” | T: and he is practising for revulsion towards consciousness of the present, for its fading away and cessation.”
sn22.9:1.19 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.104
sn35.104:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthī.
sn35.104:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach you an exposition of the teaching, an explanation of one who has found sanctuary from the yoke. | T: “Bhikkhus, I will teach you a Dhamma exposition on the theme of the one who declares the exertion to become secure from bondage.
sn35.104:1.3 | S: Listen … | T: Listen to that….
sn35.104:1.4 | S: And what is an exposition of the teaching, an explanation of one who has found sanctuary from the yoke? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is the Dhamma exposition on the theme of the one who declares the exertion to become secure from bondage?
sn35.104:1.5 | S: There are sights known by the eye, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: There are, bhikkhus, forms cognizable by the eye that are desirable, lovely, agreeable, pleasing, sensually enticing, tantalizing.
sn35.104:1.6 | S: The Realized One has given these up, cut them off at the root, made them like a palm stump, and obliterated them, so they are unable to arise in the future. | T: These have been abandoned by the Tathagata, cut off at the root, made like a palm stump, obliterated so that they are no more subject to future arising.
sn35.104:1.7 | S: He teaches yoking oneself to meditation for giving them up. That’s why the Realized One is called one who has found sanctuary from the yoke. … | T: He declares an exertion should be made for their abandoning. Therefore the Tathagata is called one who declares the exertion to become secure from bondage.
sn35.104:1.8 | S: There are ideas known by the mind, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: “There are, bhikkhus, sounds cognizable by the ear … mental phenomena cognizable by the mind that are desirable, lovely, agreeable, pleasing, sensually enticing, tantalizing.
sn35.104:1.9 | S: The Realized One has given these up, cut them off at the root, made them like a palm stump, and obliterated them, so they are unable to arise in the future. | T: These have been abandoned by the Tathagata, cut off at the root, made like a palm stump, obliterated so that they are no more subject to future arising.
sn35.104:1.10 | S: He teaches yoking oneself to meditation for giving them up. That’s why the Realized One is called one who has found sanctuary from the yoke. | T: He declares an exertion should be made for their abandoning. Therefore the Tathagata is called one who declares the exertion to become secure from bondage.
sn35.104:1.11 | S: This is an exposition of the teaching, an explanation of one who has found sanctuary from the yoke.” | T: “This, bhikkhus, is the Dhamma exposition on the theme of the one who declares the exertion to become secure from bondage.”
sn35.104:1.12 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.109
sn35.109:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach you the things that tighten the fetters, and the fetter. | T: “Bhikkhus, I will teach you the things that fetter and the fetter.
sn35.109:1.2 | S: Listen … | T: Listen to that…
sn35.109:1.3 | S: What are the things that tighten the fetters? And what is the fetter? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, are the things that fetter, and what is the fetter?
sn35.109:1.4 | S: The eye is something that tightens the fetters. | T: The eye, bhikkhus, is a thing that fetters;
sn35.109:1.5 | S: The desire and greed for it is the fetter. | T: the desire and lust for it is the fetter there.
sn35.109:1.6 | S: The ear … nose … tongue … body … | T: The ear is a thing that fetters …
sn35.109:1.7 | S: mind is something that tightens the fetters. | T: The mind is a thing that fetters;
sn35.109:1.8 | S: The desire and greed for it is the fetter. | T: the desire and lust for it is the fetter there.
sn35.109:1.9 | S: These are called the things that tighten the fetters, and this is the fetter.” | T: These are called the things that fetter, and this the fetter.”
sn35.109:1.10 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.110
sn35.110:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach you the things that fuel grasping, and the grasping. | T: “Bhikkhus, I will teach you the things that can be clung to and the clinging.
sn35.110:1.2 | S: Listen … | T: Listen to that….
sn35.110:1.3 | S: What are the things that fuel grasping? And what is the grasping? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, are the things that can be clung to, and what is the clinging?
sn35.110:1.4 | S: The eye is something that fuels grasping. | T: The eye, bhikkhus, is a thing that can be clung to;
sn35.110:1.5 | S: The desire and greed for it is the grasping. | T: the desire and lust for it is the clinging there.
sn35.110:1.6 | S: The ear … nose … tongue … body … | T: The ear is a thing that can be clung to …
sn35.110:1.7 | S: mind is something that fuels grasping. | T: The mind is a thing that can be clung to;
sn35.110:1.8 | S: The desire and greed for it is the grasping. | T: the desire and lust for it is the clinging there.
sn35.110:1.9 | S: These are called the things that fuel grasping, and this is the grasping.” | T: These are called the things that can be clung to, and this the clinging.”
sn35.110:1.10 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.116
sn35.116:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, I say it’s not possible to know or see or reach the end of the world by traveling. | T: “Bhikkhus, I say that the end of the world cannot be known, seen, or reached by travelling.
sn35.116:1.2 | S: But I also say there’s no making an end of suffering without reaching the end of the world.” | T: Yet, bhikkhus, I also say that without reaching the end of the world there is no making an end to suffering.”
sn35.116:1.3 | S: When he had spoken, the Blessed One rose from his seat and entered his dwelling. | T: Having said this, the Blessed One rose from his seat and entered his dwelling.
sn35.116:1.4 | S: Soon after the Buddha left, those mendicants considered, | T: Then, soon after the Blessed One had left, the bhikkhus considered:
sn35.116:1.5 | S: “The Buddha gave this brief summary recital, then entered his dwelling without explaining the meaning in detail. … | T: “Now, friends, the Blessed One has risen from his seat and entered his dwelling after reciting a synopsis in brief without expounding the meaning in detail.
sn35.116:1.6 | P: ‘“nāhaṁ, bhikkhave, gamanena lokassa antaṁ ñāteyyaṁ, daṭṭheyyaṁ, patteyyan”ti vadāmi. | T: (none)
sn35.116:1.7 | P: Na ca panāhaṁ, bhikkhave, appatvā lokassa antaṁ dukkhassa antakiriyaṁ vadāmī’ti. | T: (none)
sn35.116:1.8 | S: Who can explain in detail the meaning of this brief summary recital given by the Buddha?” | T: Now who will expound in detail the meaning of the synopsis that the Blessed One recited in brief?”
sn35.116:2.1 | S: Then those mendicants thought, | T: Then they considered:
sn35.116:2.2 | S: “This Venerable Ānanda is praised by the Buddha and esteemed by his sensible spiritual companions. | T: “The Venerable Ānanda is praised by the Teacher and esteemed by his wise brothers in the holy life;
sn35.116:2.3 | S: He is capable of explaining in detail the meaning of this brief summary recital given by the Buddha. | T: the Venerable Ānanda is capable of expounding in detail the meaning of this synopsis recited in brief by the Blessed One without expounding the meaning in detail.
sn35.116:2.4 | S: Let’s go to him, and ask him about this matter.” | T: Let us approach him and ask him the meaning of this.”
sn35.116:3.1 | S: Then those mendicants went to Ānanda and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then those bhikkhus approached the Venerable Ānanda and exchanged greetings with him,
sn35.116:3.2 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, they sat down to one side. They told him what had happened, and said, | T: after which they sat down to one side and told him what had taken place, adding:

