sn35.116:4.13 | S: “May Venerable Ānanda please explain this.” | T: “Let the Venerable Ānanda expound it to us.” The Venerable Ānanda replied:
sn35.116:5.1 | S: “Reverends, suppose there was a person in need of heartwood. And while wandering in search of heartwood he’d come across a large tree standing with heartwood. But he’d pass over the roots and trunk, imagining that the heartwood should be sought in the branches and leaves. | T: “Friends, it is as though a man needing heartwood, seeking heartwood, wandering in search of heartwood, would pass over the root and trunk of a great tree standing possessed of heartwood, thinking that heartwood should be sought among the branches and foliage.
sn35.116:5.2 | S: Such is the consequence for the venerables. Though you were face to face with the Buddha, you bypassed him, imagining that you should ask me about this matter. | T: And so it is with you venerable ones: when you were face to face with the Teacher you passed by the Blessed One, thinking that I should be asked about the meaning.
sn35.116:5.3 | S: For he is the Buddha, the one who knows and sees. He is vision, he is knowledge, he is the manifestation of principle, he is the manifestation of divinity. He is the preacher, the proclaimer, the elucidator of meaning, the bestower of freedom from death, the lord of truth, the Realized One. | T: For, friends, knowing, the Blessed One knows; seeing, he sees; he has become vision, he has become knowledge, he has become the Dhamma, he has become the holy one; he is the expounder, the proclaimer, the elucidator of meaning,
sn35.116:5.4 | P: cakkhubhūto, ñāṇabhūto, dhammabhūto, brahmabhūto, vattā, pavattā, atthassa ninnetā, amatassa dātā, dhammassāmī, tathāgato. | T: the giver of the Deathless, the lord of the Dhamma, the Tathagata.
sn35.116:5.5 | S: That was the time to approach the Buddha and ask about this matter. | T: That was the time when you should have asked the Blessed One the meaning.
sn35.116:5.6 | S: You should have remembered it in line with the Buddha’s answer.” | T: As he explained it to you, so you should have remembered it.”
sn35.116:6.1 | S: “Certainly he is the Buddha, the one who knows and sees. He is vision, he is knowledge, he is the manifestation of principle, he is the manifestation of divinity. He is the preacher, the proclaimer, the elucidator of meaning, the bestower of freedom from death, the lord of truth, the Realized One. | T: “Surely, friend Ānanda, knowing, the Blessed One knows; seeing, he sees; he has become vision … the Tathagata.
sn35.116:6.2 | P: cakkhubhūto, ñāṇabhūto, dhammabhūto, brahmabhūto, vattā, pavattā, atthassa ninnetā, amatassa dātā, dhammassāmī, tathāgato. | T: (none)
sn35.116:6.3 | S: That was the time to approach the Buddha and ask about this matter. | T: That was the time when we should have asked the Blessed One the meaning,
sn35.116:6.4 | S: We should have remembered it in line with the Buddha’s answer. | T: and as he explained it to us, so we should have remembered it.
sn35.116:6.5 | S: Still, Venerable Ānanda is praised by the Buddha and esteemed by his sensible spiritual companions. | T: Yet the Venerable Ānanda is praised by the Teacher and esteemed by his wise brothers in the holy life;
sn35.116:6.6 | S: You are capable of explaining in detail the meaning of this brief summary recital given by the Buddha. | T: the Venerable Ānanda is capable of expounding the detailed meaning of this synopsis recited in brief by the Blessed One without expounding the meaning in detail.
sn35.116:6.7 | S: Please explain this, if it’s no trouble.” | T: Let the Venerable Ānanda expound it without finding it troublesome.”
sn35.116:7.1 | S: “Then listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: “Then listen, friends, and attend closely to what I shall say.”
sn35.116:7.2 | S: “Yes, reverend,” they replied. | T: “Yes, friend,” the bhikkhus replied.
sn35.116:7.3 | S: Ānanda said this: | T: The Venerable Ānanda said this:
sn35.116:8.1 | S: “Reverends, the Buddha gave this brief summary recital, then entered his dwelling without explaining the meaning in detail: | T: “Friends, when the Blessed One rose from his seat and entered his dwelling after reciting a synopsis in brief without expounding the meaning in detail, that is:
sn35.116:8.2 | S: ‘Mendicants, I say it’s not possible to know or see or reach the end of the world by traveling. | T: ‘Bhikkhus, I say that the end of the world cannot be known, seen, or reached by travelling.
sn35.116:8.3 | S: But I also say there’s no making an end of suffering without reaching the end of the world.’ | T: Yet, bhikkhus, I also say that without reaching the end of the world there is no making an end to suffering,’
sn35.116:8.4 | S: This is how I understand the detailed meaning of this summary recital. | T: I understand the detailed meaning of this synopsis as follows:
sn35.116:8.5 | S: Whatever in the world through which you perceive the world and conceive the world | T: That in the world by which one is a perceiver of the world, a conceiver of the world—
sn35.116:8.6 | S: is called the world in the training of the Noble One. | T: this is called the world in the Noble One’s Discipline.
sn35.116:8.7 | S: And through what in the world do you perceive the world and conceive the world? | T: And what, friends, is that in the world by which one is a perceiver of the world, a conceiver of the world?
sn35.116:8.8 | S: Through the eye in the world you perceive the world and conceive the world. | T: The eye is that in the world by which one is a perceiver of the world, a conceiver of the world .
sn35.116:8.9 | S: Through the ear … | T: The ear …
sn35.116:8.10 | S: nose … | T: The nose …
sn35.116:8.11 | S: tongue … | T: The tongue …
sn35.116:8.12 | S: body … | T: The body …
sn35.116:8.13 | S: mind in the world you perceive the world and conceive the world. | T: The mind is that in the world by which one is a perceiver of the world, a conceiver of the world.
sn35.116:8.14 | S: Whatever in the world through which you perceive the world and conceive the world | T: That in the world by which one is a perceiver of the world, a conceiver of the world—
sn35.116:8.15 | S: is called the world in the training of the Noble One. | T: this is called the world in the Noble One’s Discipline.
sn35.116:8.16 | S: Reverends, the Buddha gave this brief summary, then entered his dwelling without explaining the meaning in detail: | T: “Friends, when the Blessed One rose from his seat and entered his dwelling after reciting a synopsis in brief without expounding the meaning in detail, that is:
sn35.116:8.17 | S: ‘Mendicants, I say it’s not possible to know or see or reach the end of the world by traveling. | T: ‘Bhikkhus, I say that the end of the world cannot be known, seen, or reached by travelling.
sn35.116:8.18 | S: But I also say there’s no making an end of suffering without reaching the end of the world.’ | T: Yet, bhikkhus, I also say that without reaching the end of the world there is no making an end to suffering,’
sn35.116:8.19 | S: That is how I understand the detailed meaning of this summary. | T: I understand the meaning of this synopsis in detail to be thus.
sn35.116:8.20 | S: If you wish, you may go to the Buddha and ask him about this. | T: Now, friends, if you wish, go to the Blessed One and ask him about the meaning of this.
sn35.116:8.21 | S: You should remember it in line with the Buddha’s answer.” | T: As the Blessed One explains it to you, so you should remember it.”
sn35.116:9.1 | S: “Yes, reverend,” replied those mendicants. Then they rose from their seats and went to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him what had happened. | T: “Yes, friends,” those bhikkhus replied, and having risen from their seats, they went to the Blessed One. After paying homage to him, they sat down to one side and told the Blessed One all that had taken place after he had left, adding:
