sn35.116:10.13 | P: Atha kho mayaṁ, bhante, yenāyasmā ānando tenupasaṅkamimha; upasaṅkamitvā āyasmantaṁ ānandaṁ etamatthaṁ paṭipucchimha. | T: “Then, venerable sir, we approached the Venerable Ānanda and asked him about the meaning.
sn35.116:10.14 | S: Then they said, “And Ānanda explained the meaning to us in this manner, with these words and phrases.” | T: The Venerable Ānanda expounded the meaning to us in these ways, with these terms, with these phrases.”
sn35.116:11.1 | S: “Mendicants, Ānanda is astute, he has great wisdom. | T: “Ānanda is wise, bhikkhus, Ānanda has great wisdom.
sn35.116:11.2 | S: If you came to me and asked this question, I would answer it in exactly the same way as Ānanda. | T: If you had asked me the meaning of this, I would have explained it to you in the same way that it has been explained by Ānanda.
sn35.116:11.3 | S: That is what it means, and that’s how you should remember it.” | T: Such is the meaning of this, and so you should remember it.”
sn35.116:11.4 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.120
sn35.120:1.1 | S: At one time Venerable Sāriputta was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: On one occasion the Venerable Sāriputta was dwelling at Savatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anathapiṇḍika’s Park.
sn35.120:1.2 | S: Then a certain mendicant went up to Venerable Sāriputta, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then a certain bhikkhu approached the Venerable Sāriputta and exchanged greetings with him.
sn35.120:1.3 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side, and said to him, | T: When they had concluded their greetings and cordial talk, he sat down to one side and said to the Venerable Sāriputta:
sn35.120:1.4 | S: “Reverend Sāriputta, a mendicant protégé of mine has resigned the training and returned to a lesser life.” | T: “Friend Sāriputta, a bhikkhu who was my co-resident has given up the training and returned to the lower life.”
sn35.120:2.1 | S: “That’s how it is, reverend, when someone doesn’t guard the sense doors, eats too much, and is not committed to wakefulness. | T: “So it is, friend, when one does not guard the doors of the sense faculties, is immoderate in eating, and is not devoted to wakefulness.
sn35.120:2.2 | S: It is quite impossible for such a mendicant to maintain the full and pure spiritual life for the rest of their life. | T: That a bhikkhu who does not guard the doors of the sense faculties, who is immoderate in eating, and who is not devoted to wakefulness will maintain all his life the complete and pure holy life—this is impossible.
sn35.120:2.3 | S: But it is quite possible for a mendicant to maintain the full and pure spiritual life for the rest of their life if they guard the sense doors, eat in moderation, and are committed to wakefulness. | T: But, friend, that a bhikkhu who guards the doors of the sense faculties, who is moderate in eating, and who is devoted to wakefulness will maintain all his life the complete and pure holy life—this is possible.
sn35.120:3.1 | S: And how does someone guard the sense doors? | T: “And how, friend, does one guard the doors of the sense faculties?
sn35.120:3.2 | S: When a mendicant sees a sight with the eyes, they don’t get caught up in the features and details. | T: Here, having seen a form with the eye, a bhikkhu does not grasp its signs and features.
sn35.120:3.3 | S: If the faculty of sight were left unrestrained, bad unskillful qualities of covetousness and displeasure would become overwhelming. For this reason, they practice restraint, protecting the faculty of sight, and achieving its restraint. | T: Since, if he left the eye faculty unrestrained, evil unwholesome states of covetousness and displeasure might invade him, he practises the way of its restraint, he guards the eye faculty, he undertakes the restraint of the eye faculty.
sn35.120:3.4 | S: When they hear a sound with their ears … | T: Having heard a sound with the ear …
sn35.120:3.5 | S: When they smell an odor with their nose … | T: Having smelt an odour with the nose …
sn35.120:3.6 | S: When they taste a flavor with their tongue … | T: Having savoured a taste with the tongue …
sn35.120:3.7 | S: When they feel a touch with their body … | T: Having felt a tactile object with the body …
sn35.120:3.8 | S: When they know an idea with their mind, they don’t get caught up in the features and details. | T: Having cognized a mental phenomenon with the mind, a bhikkhu does not grasp its signs and features.
sn35.120:3.9 | S: If the faculty of mind were left unrestrained, bad unskillful qualities of covetousness and displeasure would become overwhelming. For this reason, they practice restraint, protecting the faculty of mind, and achieving its restraint. | T: Since, if he left the mind faculty unrestrained, evil unwholesome states of covetousness and displeasure might invade him, he practises the way of its restraint, he guards the mind faculty, he undertakes the restraint of the mind faculty.
sn35.120:3.10 | S: That’s how someone guards the sense doors. | T: It is in this way, friend, that one guards the doors of the sense faculties.
sn35.120:4.1 | S: And how does someone eat in moderation? | T: “And how, friend, is one moderate in eating?
sn35.120:4.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant reflects rationally on the food that they eat: | T: Here, reflecting carefully,
sn35.120:4.3 | S: ‘Not for fun, indulgence, adornment, or decoration, but only to sustain this body, to avoid harm, and to support spiritual practice. In this way, I shall put an end to old discomfort and not give rise to new discomfort, and I will have the means to keep going, blamelessness, and a comfortable abiding.’ | T: a bhikkhu takes food neither for amusement nor for intoxication nor for the sake of physical beauty and attractiveness, but only for the support and maintenance of this body, for ending discomfort, and for assisting the holy life, considering: ‘Thus I shall terminate the old feeling and not arouse a new feeling, and I shall be healthy and blameless and live in comfort.’
sn35.120:4.4 | S: That’s how someone eats in moderation. | T: It is in this way, friend, that one is moderate in eating.
sn35.120:5.1 | S: And how is someone committed to wakefulness? | T: “And how, friend, is one devoted to wakefulness?
sn35.120:5.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant practices walking and sitting meditation by day, purifying their mind from obstacles. | T: Here, during the day, while walking back and forth and sitting, a bhikkhu purifies his mind of obstructive states.
sn35.120:5.3 | S: In the first watch of the night, they continue to practice walking and sitting meditation. | T: In the first watch of the night, while walking back and forth and sitting, he purifies his mind of obstructive states.
sn35.120:5.4 | S: In the middle watch, they lie down in the lion’s posture—on the right side, placing one foot on top of the other—mindful and aware, and focused on the time of getting up. | T: In the middle watch of the night he lies down on his right side in the lion’s posture with one foot overlapping the other, mindful and clearly comprehending, after noting in his mind the idea of rising.
sn35.120:5.5 | S: In the last watch, they get up and continue to practice walking and sitting meditation, purifying their mind from obstacles. | T: After rising, in the last watch of the night, while walking back and forth and sitting, he purifies his mind of obstructive states.
sn35.120:5.6 | S: That’s how someone is committed to wakefulness. | T: It is in this way, friend, that one is devoted to wakefulness.
sn35.120:5.7 | S: So you should train like this: | T: “Therefore, friend, you should train yourself thus:
sn35.120:5.8 | S: ‘We will guard the sense doors, eat in moderation, and be committed to wakefulness.’ | T: ‘We will guard the doors of the sense faculties; we will be moderate in eating; we will be devoted to wakefulness.’
sn35.120:5.9 | S: That’s how you should train.” | T: Thus, friend, should you train yourself.”
sn35.120:5.10 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)


